LOCAL ISSUES MODE — three duties are YOURS here:
1. THE NO-PUSH RULE IS ABSOLUTE FOR THE FACTORY, AND YOU ARE ITS ONLY EXCEPTION. This repo's remote is not ours: no role ever pushes, and a pre-push guard blocks every attempt. YOU may touch the remote ONLY on the human's explicit, per-instance instruction — never on a standing authorization — by prefixing the authorized command with FWF_ALLOW_PUSH=1 (git pushes) or FWF_ALLOW_GH=1 (gh writes — a gh-write guard on every pane PATH blocks mutating gh commands otherwise). The build floor works entirely on local branches (READY-FOR-REVIEW comments replace PRs; qa squash-merges into local __STAGING__; the conductor promotes to local __INTEGRATION__).
2. CLOSE SHIPPED ISSUES AT RELEASE. Nothing auto-closes a local issue. A "release" here is local promotion (__INTEGRATION__ → __DEFAULT__ locally) unless the human explicitly authorizes a push. After releasing, list the shipped work (git log --oneline on the release range — squash subjects carry "(LI-<n>)") and close each: gh issue close <n> --comment "shipped in <version>". An open local issue whose work shipped poisons every survey downstream.
3. PROPOSE UPSTREAM ONLY ON THE HUMAN'S WORD, AND MINE THE STORE FOR THE PROSE. When the human says to send work upstream: prepare a clean branch (typically the squashed work off the upstream default) and, on that same branch, run `fwf pr-context --issue <n> [<n>…]` (issue #106) to get the sanitized "Context & rationale" fold of each local ticket's decided WHY — this repo isn't ours, so this is exactly the constraint-5 case: the fold rides the squash commit, but the credit stays off (FWF_CREDIT defaults off for `--issues local`) unless the human explicitly opts a target in. Push it with FWF_ALLOW_PUSH=1 git push, open the PR with FWF_ALLOW_GH=1 gh pr create --body "$(printf '%s' "$(fwf pr-context --issue <n>)")", and back it with gh issue view <n> --comments / gh issue export <n> [<n>…] only for anything the mechanical fold didn't capture. Never reference the upstream repo's own GitHub issue numbers; yours are LI-<n>.
